00:22:17.254 --> 00:22:18.630
Did you know that him and his girlfriend

479
00:22:18.714 --> 00:22:20.174
just decided to open up
their relationship?

480
00:22:20.257 --> 00:22:21.425
Yes. I know.

481
00:22:21.508 --> 00:22:23.260
I know that his father
went back to college.

482
00:22:23.343 --> 00:22:25.345
I know that his sister is bisexual.

483
00:22:25.429 --> 00:22:27.347
I know everything there is to know
about Ryan.

484
00:22:27.431 --> 00:22:31.185
And I know that I wish Ryan and I
just had a little more mystery between us.

485
00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:33.020
Wait. "dazzle me moi"?

486
00:22:33.103 --> 00:22:34.688
Come on now.

487
00:22:35.272 --> 00:22:36.565
Oh...

488
00:22:40.194 --> 00:22:42.112
Oh. my God.

489
00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:44.740
Oh.

490
00:22:44.823 --> 00:22:46.867
Like disco balls for your feet.

491
00:22:48.076 --> 00:22:49.161
I hope they have the other one.

492
00:22:51.288 --> 00:22:52.289
Yeah. that's nice.

493
00:22:52.372 --> 00:22:55.250
If it didn't have all the bits
and bobs on it. Who wears this shit?

494
00:22:55.334 --> 00:22:56.960
Good morning. ladies.

495
00:22:57.044 --> 00:22:58.545
- Hi.
- Hi. hello.

496
00:22:58.629 --> 00:23:00.005
I'm Tammy Dineen.

497
00:23:00.088 --> 00:23:03.258
So let me know if you two life-size Barbie
dolls need a hand with anything. okay?

498
00:23:03.342 --> 00:23:04.426
Thank you.

499
00:23:08.013 --> 00:23:09.389
She didn't hear me. did she?

500
00:23:23.737 --> 00:23:24.738
Is this. uh...

501
00:23:25.781 --> 00:23:26.782
Oh.

502
00:23:29.326 --> 00:23:31.537
Ooh. Hi. um. excuse me.

503
00:23:31.620 --> 00:23:34.164
- Will the jazz museum be opening shortly?
- No. sir.

504
00:23:34.248 --> 00:23:35.832
It's closed for a private event.

505
00:23:35.916 --> 00:23:38.752
- Oh.
- The Abramowitz Bar Mitzvah.

506
00:23:38.836 --> 00:23:42.130
Rumor is the parents hired
Janelle Monáe. Ariana Grande.

507
00:23:42.214 --> 00:23:45.634
and. uh. someone else real tiny.

508
00:23:45.717 --> 00:23:46.802
Bruno Mars?

509
00:23:47.553 --> 00:23:49.012
- That's the one.
- Ah.

510
00:23:49.555 --> 00:23:51.723
- You can come in here if you want.
- Uh. What's this?

511
00:23:51.807 --> 00:23:54.101
Uh. it's a museum dedicated
to Black baseball players.

512
00:23:54.184 --> 00:23:55.477
Oh.

513
00:23:55.561 --> 00:23:57.396
Yeah. it's not for everyone.

514
00:23:57.479 --> 00:23:59.565
Oh. no. no. no. I-I love Black people.

515
00:23:59.648 --> 00:24:01.275
- Hmm.
- I mean. not all of them.

516
00:24:01.358 --> 00:24:02.943
- Hmm.
- O-Only 'cause you can't love

517
00:24:03.026 --> 00:24:04.111
all of anything. right?

518
00:24:05.362 --> 00:24:08.156
I-I mean. I-I just don't know anything
about baseball.

519
00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:10.284
It-It's like cricket. right?

520
00:24:10.367 --> 00:24:13.287
'Cause you both use hard white balls.

521
00:24:13.370 --> 00:24:15.080
You know what. I mean.
I'd love to.

522
00:24:15.163 --> 00:24:17.708
- That'd be ten dollars. please.
- Yes. of course. I am so sorry.

523
00:24:17.791 --> 00:24:19.334
Yes. Yeah. Yeah.

524
00:24:33.098 --> 00:24:35.267
Hey. uh. Dottie.
the tag here says ten bucks.

525
00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:36.643
How about five? Huh?

526
00:24:36.727 --> 00:24:41.565
Oh. boy. I brought that back from Italy.
gosh. 30 years ago now.

527
00:24:42.149 --> 00:24:44.651
Handmade by a Vatican priest.

528
00:24:44.735 --> 00:24:47.237
- I got to have it. Here's 20.
- What?

529
00:24:47.321 --> 00:24:48.989
- Oh.
- What are you doing?

530
00:24:49.656 --> 00:24:50.824
Babe. I was doing something.

531
00:24:51.408 --> 00:24:52.910
You gotta let me haggle.

532
00:24:52.993 --> 00:24:55.829
Mom. Michelle and I gave you that lamp
five years ago for Mother's Day.

533
00:24:55.913 --> 00:25:00.334
Well. like the man once said.
hardest part of sales is the lying.

534
00:25:01.877 --> 00:25:03.754
Here's all that stuff
Henry wanted to sell.

535
00:25:03.837 --> 00:25:06.715
Uh. he said he'll only take 20%.
You can have the rest.

536
00:25:06.798 --> 00:25:08.467
Sweet kid. I'll give him 12.

537
00:25:09.218 --> 00:25:11.094
I will let him know.
All right. I should get going.

538
00:25:11.178 --> 00:25:13.180
Oh... actually.
will you price all this stuff

539
00:25:13.263 --> 00:25:15.182
before you go? Four bucks apiece.

540
00:25:15.724 --> 00:25:18.227
All right. fine. Um...

541
00:25:19.603 --> 00:25:20.604
What...

542
00:25:21.688 --> 00:25:24.775
- What the heck?
- That can be yours for five dollars.

543
00:25:24.858 --> 00:25:25.984
Yeah. but I gave it to you.

544
00:25:26.818 --> 00:25:27.819
Ten dollars.

545
00:25:27.903 --> 00:25:29.738
It was a gift from the head coach.

546
00:25:30.531 --> 00:25:32.783
Oh. uh. hey.
Fun fact about this mixing bowl.

547
00:25:34.576 --> 00:25:36.995
And I know the women
can't have a team plane yet.

548
00:25:37.079 --> 00:25:38.872
but what about a team train?

549
00:25:38.956 --> 00:25:41.792
With an ice bath and massage tables
for the team

550
00:25:41.875 --> 00:25:45.045
and a fancy caboose with a bar
for the owner and her best mate.

551
00:25:45.128 --> 00:25:47.506
Keeley. uh...
There's something I need to tell you.

552
00:25:47.589 --> 00:25:48.966
- Y'all doing okay?
- I ju...

553
00:25:49.049 --> 00:25:51.176
Yes. she's just trying on the suit
that you got for her.

554
00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:53.637
Did I get your size right.
Glamazon with pajamas on?

555
00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:58.058
Uh. yes. I just...
I'm just not sure it's me.

556
00:25:58.141 --> 00:25:59.268
Well. like I always say.

557
00:25:59.351 --> 00:26:02.437
wearing new clothes
is like a vacation for your body.

558
00:26:02.521 --> 00:26:04.231
Ooh. I like that.

559
00:26:04.731 --> 00:26:06.859
Come on. get on out here
and show us what you got.

560
00:26:06.942 --> 00:26:08.318
Yeah. come on. Rebecca. Let's see.

561
00:26:08.402 --> 00:26:09.987
Mm-hmm.

562
00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:13.740
Well. consider <i>moi</i> dazzled.

563
00:26:13.824 --> 00:26:15.409
- Oh. you look incredible.
- Yeah. no. no.

564
00:26:15.492 --> 00:26:17.536
- it's-it's. uh. quite nice.
- Mmm.

565
00:26:17.619 --> 00:26:19.788
Oh. I love this place.
Is it all yours. Tammy?

566
00:26:19.872 --> 00:26:21.999
Oh. yes. ma'am. I used the cash
from my divorce settlement.

567
00:26:22.082 --> 00:26:23.876
Figured it's about time to be my own boss.

568
00:26:23.959 --> 00:26:26.503
The only thing I didn't consider
was a goddamn recession.

569
00:26:26.587 --> 00:26:28.213
But luckily. a couple of summers ago.

570
00:26:28.297 --> 00:26:30.090
when Taylor Swift was performing in town.

571
00:26:30.174 --> 00:26:33.719
one of her backup singers.
a wingless angel of color.

572
00:26:33.802 --> 00:26:36.263
she floated in. bought an outfit.
posted it on her Insta.

573
00:26:36.847 --> 00:26:39.308
We've been popping and hopping.
and gals have been shopping ever since.

574
00:26:39.391 --> 00:26:40.684
Women helping women. am I right?

575
00:26:44.062 --> 00:26:45.314
So what do you think. Rebecca?

576
00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:48.650
- Oh. God. I'll take it.
- Yes!

577
00:26:48.734 --> 00:26:50.694
Looks. brains. and good taste.

578
00:26:51.987 --> 00:26:53.238
She is beautiful.

579
00:26:53.322 --> 00:26:54.656
I'm obsessed.

580
00:27:08.086 --> 00:27:12.007
Satchel Paige.
the biggest star of the Negro Leagues.

581
00:27:12.633 --> 00:27:16.595
Some say he is
the greatest pitcher of all time.

582
00:27:16.678 --> 00:27:21.475
better than Cy Young. Sandy Koufax.
Nolan Ryan.

583
00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:23.685
Well. those are certainly some names...

584
00:27:23.769 --> 00:27:25.062
- Mm-hmm.
- Of baseball men.

585
00:27:25.145 --> 00:27:30.317
In your terms.
think Duke Ellington. John Coltrane.

586
00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:32.653
and Louis Armstrong all rolled into one.

587
00:27:32.736 --> 00:27:34.988
Whoa. Well. blow my top. Daddy-o.

588
00:27:35.072 --> 00:27:36.657
Uh. now you get it.

589
00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:38.534
<i>See. Satchel was a showman.</i>

590
00:27:38.617 --> 00:27:42.579
<i>He had a wild throwing style
and all these crazy names for his pitches.</i>

591
00:27:42.663 --> 00:27:46.291
<i>The Hesitation. the Midnight Creeper.
the Dipsy-Do.</i>

592
00:27:46.375 --> 00:27:50.045
<i>Many thought old Satch would be the first
to break the Major League color barrier.</i>

593
00:27:50.629 --> 00:27:51.755
<i>He wasn't.</i>

594
00:27:51.839 --> 00:27:53.423
<i>Manager Dressen
thanks Hero Robinson.</i>

595
00:27:53.507 --> 00:27:56.134
<i>But he finally got in
at the age of 42.</i>

596
00:27:56.218 --> 00:27:58.554
<i>And in comes
Cleveland's Satchel Paige.</i>

597
00:27:58.637 --> 00:28:03.308
<i>Oldest rookie in history.
and he was still incredible.</i>

598
00:28:03.392 --> 00:28:06.019
<i>Yeah. the Negro Leagues were glorious.</i>

599
00:28:06.687 --> 00:28:09.314
<i>They didn't do it for fame or fortune.</i>

600
00:28:09.398 --> 00:28:12.818
<i>They did it because to not play
would have been unacceptable.</i>

601
00:28:13.402 --> 00:28:15.779
My favorite saying of old Satch's was.

602
00:28:15.863 --> 00:28:18.574
"Ain't no man can avoid
being born average.

603
00:28:19.283 --> 00:28:21.410
But there ain't no man
that got to be common."

604
00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:25.080
- Hmm.
- Look around.

605
00:28:25.747 --> 00:28:26.874
You see it.

606
00:28:31.128 --> 00:28:33.255
None of these men were common.

607
00:28:36.967 --> 00:28:38.302
They're immortal.

608
00:28:38.385 --> 00:28:39.887
- Mm-hmm.
- Hmm.

609
00:28:45.017 --> 00:28:49.104
Oh. You're a natural.

610
00:28:49.188 --> 00:28:50.480
Thank you. Charles.

611
00:28:50.564 --> 00:28:53.150
You know. their story is quite similar

612
00:28:53.233 --> 00:28:56.820
to how women in England weren't allowed
to play football for years.

613
00:28:56.904 --> 00:28:58.030
Mmm.

614
00:28:58.113 --> 00:28:59.156
Not similar.

615
00:28:59.239 --> 00:29:01.742
Sorry. uh. what happened here. much worse.

616
00:29:01.825 --> 00:29:04.620
Much. much worse. Much. much. much. much.

617
00:29:04.703 --> 00:29:06.330
I can't stop saying much for some reason.

618
00:29:06.413 --> 00:29:08.123
Is there a gift shop I can support?

619
00:29:08.207 --> 00:29:11.376
It's right over here.

620
00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:13.921
So when did they start letting women
play soccer in the UK?

621
00:29:14.004 --> 00:29:15.088
1971.

622
00:29:15.172 --> 00:29:17.466
- Damn. that's recent as hell.
- I know. right?

623
00:29:18.759 --> 00:29:21.011
And you're sure all it needs is a battery?

624
00:29:21.094 --> 00:29:22.262
That's right.

625
00:29:22.346 --> 00:29:25.224
And if not.
it'll still be right twice a day.

626
00:29:26.308 --> 00:29:27.309
Hmm.

627
00:29:28.519 --> 00:29:29.770
Okay. Mom. I gotta go.

628
00:29:29.853 --> 00:29:32.564
Oh. actually. Teddy. could you bring
the box down from your bedroom?

629
00:29:32.648 --> 00:29:34.149
It was too heavy for me.

630
00:29:36.485 --> 00:29:37.569
Thank you. baby.

631
00:29:37.653 --> 00:29:41.448
I love you. my big strong baby boy.

632
00:29:41.532 --> 00:29:42.533
Mm-hmm.

633
00:30:35.878 --> 00:30:37.713
Thanks. honey.

634
00:30:39.298 --> 00:30:41.008
You remember when Dad used
to take me to his work?

635
00:30:41.091 --> 00:30:43.468
Oh. of course. You loved it.

636
00:30:43.552 --> 00:30:44.636
Yeah.

637
00:30:44.720 --> 00:30:46.889
Every time I went up there.
folks would get so excited.

638
00:30:47.639 --> 00:30:49.516
treat me like
I won a contest or something.

639
00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:52.728
Let me raid the vending machine

640
00:30:52.811 --> 00:30:55.772
or race around on those fancy
office chairs they had up there.

641
00:30:55.856 --> 00:31:00.360
Talking about what a great guy Dad was.
You know... just... it was fun.

642
00:31:01.612 --> 00:31:02.696
Yeah.

643
00:31:05.032 --> 00:31:06.658
Hey. why don't you ever ask me
about my job?

644
00:31:07.242 --> 00:31:10.704
I mean. no one does. you know?
Not you. Not Michelle. Henry.

645
00:31:11.455 --> 00:31:13.040
Well. I'm sure I have.

646
00:31:13.123 --> 00:31:14.416
- I've...
- Mom.

647
00:31:15.459 --> 00:31:19.588
Well. If you don't care about it.
why should I?

648
00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:21.381
Whoa.

649
00:31:21.465 --> 00:31:22.799
Teddy Bear.

650
00:31:22.883 --> 00:31:24.760
You didn't love going to work
with your father

651
00:31:24.843 --> 00:31:26.470
'cause of how it made you feel.

652
00:31:26.553 --> 00:31:29.431
You loved being around
how his job made him feel.

653
00:31:30.140 --> 00:31:33.977
It's good for a boy to see his father
do something he loves.

654
00:31:34.061 --> 00:31:35.103
Hmm.

655
00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:37.606
Take the job.

656
00:31:41.193 --> 00:31:42.903
For crying out loud. Mom.

657
00:31:42.986 --> 00:31:44.988
You tell me that my son needs me.
so I've got to come home.

658
00:31:45.072 --> 00:31:46.615
Now you're telling me I should go back?

659
00:31:47.241 --> 00:31:49.159
What the heck am I supposed to do.
split myself in half?

660
00:31:50.035 --> 00:31:51.286
You'll figure it out.

661
00:31:53.038 --> 00:31:55.499
Oh. hey. that is pure silk from India.

662
00:31:55.582 --> 00:31:57.543
A personal gift from Mr. Yul Brynner.

663
00:32:15.727 --> 00:32:19.398
As you may know. CPKC is the first
privately funded stadium in the world

664
00:32:19.481 --> 00:32:21.775
built solely
for professional women's soccer.

665
00:32:21.859 --> 00:32:23.402
- Hmm.
- Love that blazer. by the way.

666
00:32:23.485 --> 00:32:26.405
Oh. thank you. It's a Dazzle Me Moi.

667
00:32:26.488 --> 00:32:28.156
- Tammy's incredible.
- Hmm.

668
00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:29.241
- Hi. Katie.
- Hi.

669
00:32:29.324 --> 00:32:32.160
Ooh. I think we should
build our own stadium.

670
00:32:32.244 --> 00:32:33.704
I think we should play a match first.

671
00:32:33.787 --> 00:32:35.455
Right. yeah. of course. Yep. I know. yeah.

672
00:32:35.539 --> 00:32:37.958
Hey. Katie. A bunch of questions for you.

673
00:32:38.041 --> 00:32:41.253
It's like walking around Wonka's
chocolate factory with Veruca Salt.

674
00:32:41.795 --> 00:32:43.463
I want a stadium now.

675
00:32:43.547 --> 00:32:45.841
Well. the women's team
does need our support.

676
00:32:46.592 --> 00:32:48.886
'Cause ain't none of those women
are common. Rebecca.

677
00:32:50.596 --> 00:32:52.598
- What are you wearing?
- What are you wearing?

678
00:32:53.765 --> 00:32:54.766
Hmm. Good point.

679
00:32:56.268 --> 00:32:57.394
Hmm.

680
00:32:57.477 --> 00:32:58.562
You okay. Ted?

681
00:32:58.645 --> 00:33:01.106
Yeah. just running some laps up here
in the old noggin.

682
00:33:01.190 --> 00:33:03.525
I dig this fit. You look like Mookie
from <i>Do the Right Thing</i>.

683
00:33:03.609 --> 00:33:05.360
- Ooh. thank you.
- Mm-hmm.

684
00:33:12.326 --> 00:33:14.244
Come on! Bring it on. yes!

685
00:33:27.424 --> 00:33:30.636
Oh. my God!

686
00:33:48.445 --> 00:33:50.906
The energy here is incredible. Whoo!

687
00:33:55.869 --> 00:33:58.121
Yes-a!

688
00:34:07.714 --> 00:34:09.424
- Bye. Ted.
- Bye.

689
00:34:09.507 --> 00:34:11.217
I love Kansas City.

690
00:34:11.301 --> 00:34:13.512
- Yeah.
- Great to see how the sausage is made.

691
00:34:15.304 --> 00:34:16.849
You're the sausage.

692
00:34:16.931 --> 00:34:20.310
Oh.

693
00:34:20.393 --> 00:34:22.646
This is one of my see you soon hugs. yeah?

694
00:34:23.856 --> 00:34:25.607
My goodbye hugs last for days.

695
00:34:25.690 --> 00:34:27.818
- Oh. I remember.
- Good.

696
00:34:30.654 --> 00:34:31.697
Hello.

697
00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:33.156
Hey. Mr. Higgins.

698
00:34:33.239 --> 00:34:35.449
- Ted. I'm gonna miss you.
- Hmm.

699
00:34:35.534 --> 00:34:37.369
And I'm going to miss
the hot Midwestern air

700
00:34:37.452 --> 00:34:38.661
flowing through my leg hair.

701
00:34:38.745 --> 00:34:40.205
The air's gonna miss them calves.

702
00:34:40.289 --> 00:34:43.500
- Ah. man.
- Take good care.

703
00:34:43.583 --> 00:34:45.168
Oh boy. that's a lot of food.

704
00:34:45.252 --> 00:34:47.629
- Don't look at me. It's hers.
- Hmm.

705
00:34:47.713 --> 00:34:49.715
I hope you left some for the rest of us.

706
00:34:49.797 --> 00:34:52.717
Well. what can I say. Ted?
Kansas City barbecue's changed me.

707
00:34:52.801 --> 00:34:54.261
Hmm. Well. if you eat all that food.

708
00:34:54.344 --> 00:34:56.096
you're gonna change the toilet
on that plane.

709
00:35:00.601 --> 00:35:02.227
And. hey. I'm sorry you came out
all this way

710
00:35:02.311 --> 00:35:04.396
to not get the answer you're looking for.

711
00:35:05.689 --> 00:35:08.859
I can't take the job. I got to stay put.
This is home.

712
00:35:10.694 --> 00:35:13.655
You know. sometimes home
is just a state of mind.

713
00:35:18.702 --> 00:35:20.787
Just a little something to remember me by.

714
00:35:24.458 --> 00:35:26.043
- Thanks. boss.
- Hmm.

715
00:35:31.632 --> 00:35:34.009
Bye. Ted. God bless you.

716
00:35:35.219 --> 00:35:37.387
You better hurry
or you'll miss that plane.

717
00:35:37.471 --> 00:35:39.181
It's my fucking plane. Ted.
It's not going anywhere.

718
00:35:39.264 --> 00:35:41.141
Oh. I'm sorry. I thought
we were doing <i>Casablanca</i> here.

719
00:35:41.225 --> 00:35:42.267
- Never seen it.
- Oh.

720
00:35:42.351 --> 00:35:44.937
- Never will. Hmm.
- Good for you.

